  "activity": {
    "id": "act_retrograde_lab",
    "name": "Apparent Retrograde Motion Lab",
    "description": "An exploration of apparent retrograde motion \u2014 the puzzle of why outer planets like Mars appear to periodically reverse direction in the night sky. Students contrast the geocentric and heliocentric models and observe how Earth overtaking Mars produces the retrograde loop.",
    "language": "en",
    "workplace": "wp_retrograde",
    "start": "act_intro_nightsky",
    "actions": [
      {
        "id": "act_intro_nightsky",
        "viewport": "actions",
        "type": "actions",
        "device": "ar_headset",
        "location": "sky_dome",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "The Puzzle of the Night Sky",
          "description": "Look up at the night sky. Watch the red point of Mars as we fast-forward through six months of time. Notice how it normally drifts eastward against the constellations, but then slows down, reverses into a westward loop for about two months, and finally resumes its eastward motion. Why would a planet move backward? This apparent retrograde motion was the central puzzle of early astronomy."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "sky_dome",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "anim_mars_nightsky"
            },
            {
              "target": "learner",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "audio_intro"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": [],
          "messages": [],
          "if_logic": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "learner",
              "type": "action",
              "augmentation": "act_geocentric"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "sky_dome",
              "type": "*",
              "augmentation": "*"
            },
            {
              "target": "learner",
              "type": "*",
              "augmentation": "*"
            }
          ],
          "messages": [],
          "if_logic": []
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "ar_headset",
            "operator": "equal"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "act_geocentric",
        "viewport": "actions",
        "type": "actions",
        "device": "ar_headset",
        "location": "solar_system_table",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "The Geocentric Explanation",
          "description": "Step over to the solar system table. The ancient geocentric model placed Earth at the center. To explain the backward loop, astronomers proposed that Mars rides on a small circle (an epicycle) which in turn rides on a larger circle (the deferent). Watch the machinery run. While this reproduces the loop, it required increasingly complex and awkward adjustments as observations improved."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "solar_system_table",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "anim_geocentric"
            },
            {
              "target": "learner",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "audio_geocentric"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": [],
          "messages": [],
          "if_logic": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "learner",
              "type": "action",
              "augmentation": "act_heliocentric"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "solar_system_table",
              "type": "*",
              "augmentation": "*"
            },
            {
              "target": "learner",
              "type": "*",
              "augmentation": "*"
            }
          ],
          "messages": [],
          "if_logic": []
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "ar_headset",
            "operator": "equal"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "act_heliocentric",
        "viewport": "actions",
        "type": "actions",
        "device": "ar_headset",
        "location": "solar_system_table",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "The Heliocentric Reality",
          "description": "Now view the heliocentric (Sun-centered) model. Notice that Earth orbits at 1 AU, moving faster than Mars at 1.52 AU. Watch what happens every 26 months when Earth catches up to and overtakes Mars on the inside track. The retrograde loop isn't a complex physical spiral; it falls out naturally from the smooth, elliptical orbits of the two planets."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "solar_system_table",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "anim_heliocentric"
            },
            {
              "target": "learner",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "audio_heliocentric"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": [],
          "messages": [],
          "if_logic": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "learner",
              "type": "action",
              "augmentation": "act_side_by_side"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "solar_system_table",
              "type": "*",
              "augmentation": "*"
            },
            {
              "target": "learner",
              "type": "*",
              "augmentation": "*"
            }
          ],
          "messages": [],
          "if_logic": []
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "ar_headset",
            "operator": "equal"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "act_side_by_side",
        "viewport": "actions",
        "type": "actions",
        "device": "ar_headset",
        "location": "solar_system_table",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Connecting the Viewpoints",
          "description": "Let's link the two perspectives. Follow the line drawn from Earth, through Mars, extending out to the background stars. As Earth overtakes Mars, this line of sight naturally sweeps westward for a brief time before continuing eastward. The retrograde loop is a real apparent motion caused by relative geometry, just like a slower car appearing to drift backward when you pass it on the highway. Say 'Continue' when you are ready."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "solar_system_table",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "anim_side_by_side"
            },
            {
              "target": "learner",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "audio_sidebyside"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": [],
          "messages": [],
          "if_logic": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
